userContent.text:
Build an interactive figure using Chart.js.

CONCEPT: The same slowly varying message signal can be encoded onto a fast radio carrier either by varying the carrier's amplitude (AM) or by varying its frequency (FM).
KEY INSIGHT (what students should walk away understanding): A wave has several independent properties, and any one of them can carry a message: AM writes the signal into the carrier's height, FM writes the same signal into the carrier's cycle spacing.
DEMO STEPS: 5 guided steps — implement the step player in #ui

FULL BLUEPRINT:
{
  "concept": "The same slowly varying message signal can be encoded onto a fast radio carrier either by varying the carrier's amplitude (AM) or by varying its frequency (FM).",
  "figureType": "line_plot",
  "elements": [
    "Shared horizontal time axis (implicit, no tick labels) spanning the full width for all three stacked traces",
    "Top trace: smooth black low-frequency sine-like curve with two broad crests and one central trough, labeled 'Signal' at its right end",
    "Middle trace: red high-frequency carrier with uniform peak spacing and swelling/collapsing amplitude, labeled 'AM' at its right end",
    "Dashed red envelope curves (upper and lower) tracing the AM peak heights, mirroring the black signal shape",
    "Bottom trace: blue carrier with constant amplitude and varying cycle spacing — tight below signal crests, stretched below the signal trough — labeled 'FM' at its right end",
    "Vertical time marker line crossing all three traces, with small dots at each intersection",
    "Readout box reporting signal height, AM envelope height, and FM local cycle spacing / instantaneous frequency at the marker",
    "Reconstructed (decoded) black curve drawn above the active carrier when decoding is enabled",
    "Highlight/dim styling for the active vs. inactive carrier trace",
    "Small annotation callouts: 'amplitude varies, spacing constant' on AM; 'spacing varies, amplitude constant' on FM"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "timeMarker",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Time marker position",
      "range": [
        0,
        1,
        0.005
      ],
      "default": 0.5,
      "teaches": "At any instant, the message value shows up as AM envelope height and simultaneously as FM cycle spacing — two different wave properties carrying the same number."
    },
    {
      "id": "carrierMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Decode carrier",
      "options": [
        "Compare both",
        "AM",
        "FM"
      ],
      "default": "Compare both",
      "teaches": "Isolating one carrier and rebuilding the original signal from it shows that amplitude and frequency are interchangeable channels for the same information."
    },
    {
      "id": "showEnvelope",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Show AM envelope / FM frequency track",
      "default": false,
      "teaches": "The hidden message is made visible as the outline of the AM peaks or as the instantaneous-frequency curve of FM."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The message signal",
      "narration": "The black curve at the top is the slow information waveform — two crests and a trough. Everything below it is a fast radio carrier carrying this shape.",
      "state": {
        "carrierMode": "Compare both",
        "showEnvelope": false,
        "timeMarker": 0.15
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Scan the marker",
      "narration": "Drag the time marker across the figure. Where the black signal is high, the red AM wave is tall and the blue FM cycles crowd together; where the signal dips, AM shrinks and FM spreads out.",
      "state": {
        "carrierMode": "Compare both",
        "showEnvelope": false,
        "timeMarker": 0.5
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "AM: message in the envelope",
      "narration": "Switch to AM. The peak spacing never changes, but the dashed envelope tracing the peak heights reproduces the original signal exactly.",
      "state": {
        "carrierMode": "AM",
        "showEnvelope": true,
        "timeMarker": 0.25
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "FM: message in the spacing",
      "narration": "Switch to FM. Every cycle is the same height, yet counting cycles per unit time recovers the very same black curve.",
      "state": {
        "carrierMode": "FM",
        "showEnvelope": true,
        "timeMarker": 0.5
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Same message, two encodings",
      "narration": "Compare both decoded curves: identical information, carried by two independent properties of the wave — which is why FM survives amplitude noise that would corrupt AM.",
      "state": {
        "carrierMode": "Compare both",
        "showEnvelope": true,
        "timeMarker": 0.8
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "A wave has several independent properties, and any one of them can carry a message: AM writes the signal into the carrier's height, FM writes the same signal into the carrier's cycle spacing.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Three horizontally stacked traces on a white background sharing an invisible common time axis, each labeled in black text at the right margin: 'Signal' (top, black, ~2px stroke), 'AM' (middle, brick red), 'FM' (bottom, steel blue). Top black curve is roughly one and a half slow cycles: high crest left, broad trough center, high crest right. Red AM trace has about 20 uniformly spaced cycles; amplitude is maximal under the black crests and pinches to a narrow waist under the black trough, so its peak outline copies the signal. Blue FM trace has constant amplitude throughout with cycles visibly compressed under the crests and stretched under the trough. When envelopes are on, draw dashed red mirror-image envelope curves hugging the AM peaks and a thin dashed black 'recovered signal' curve above the active carrier. A vertical gray marker line spans all three traces with small filled dots at each crossing, and a compact readout panel in a corner shows signal amplitude, AM envelope height, and FM cycle spacing at the marker. Inactive traces dim to light gray when one carrier is selected."
}

